{"questions":[{"question_id":"VcCAgyN3fplIli6M","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2003","year":2003,"examDate":2003,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"chemical-kinetics-and-nuclear-chemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","topic":"arrhenius-equation","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"in-respect-of-the-equation-k-ae-eart-in-chemical-kinetics-2003-marks-4-vccagyn3fplili6m.htm","content":"In respect of the equation k = Ae<sup>-E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup> in chemical kinetics, which one of the following statements is correct?","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"A is adsorption factor"},{"identifier":"B","content":"E<sub>a</sub> is energy of activation"},{"identifier":"C","content":"R is Rydberg’s constant"},{"identifier":"D","content":"k is equilibrium constant"}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"<p>The Arrhenius equation reads  </p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.114ex\" height=\"3.197ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1402.2 5354.4 1413.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D458\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D458\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(798.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1854.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D434\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D434\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(2604.6,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(2771.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(499,470.3) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mstyle\" transform=\"translate(778,0) scale(1.414)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(319.3,451.1) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(771,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(759,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D447\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D447\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"1243.7\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>  </p>\n<p>Let’s check each term:</p>\n\n<p><p>A is the pre-exponential (or frequency) factor, not an adsorption factor.  </p></p>\n<p><p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.355ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.704ex\" height=\"1.894ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 1195.1 837.1\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(771,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is indeed the activation energy.  </p></p>\n<p><p>R is the universal gas constant (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.542ex\" height=\"1.581ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -677 3333.8 699\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1055.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"38\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-38\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\" transform=\"translate(1778,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>\\,J·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹), not Rydberg’s constant.  </p></p>\n<p><p>k is the rate constant, not the equilibrium constant.  </p></p>\n\n<p>Hence, the correct statement is:  </p>\n<p><strong>Option B: <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.355ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.704ex\" height=\"1.894ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -680 1195.1 837.1\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D438\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D438\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(771,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is energy of activation.</strong></p>"}]}